<p>I have a few nitpicks - sorry for not picking up in the drafts:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Expand</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>a temple complex with an area of approximately 20&#160;m<sup>2</sup></p>
</blockquote>
<p>While a 4m x 5m structure isn't a small shrine, I have difficulty accepting it as a temple complex with a central chamber. Even going up to 200m<sup>2</sup> would still only be around 20m x 10m, which is small enough to hide on the island, and you wouldn't have to make it that large.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A sign in front of SCP-2248 labels the site as &quot;Templum Luna Primogenita&quot;.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This was jarring both from the use of the phrase &quot;sign in front&quot; (which suggests a museum label - I'm not aware of that kind of practice in Roman temples generally) and the idea that a persecuted cult wanting to worship their sacred statue in secret would stick a sign on the front for helpful reference.</p>
<p>One possible solution is to delete the sentence entirely. It's clear by the end of the draft what the temple is. If you want to give readers a helpful signpost, however, perhaps you could replace the sign in front of the temple with an inscription on the temple (&quot;Gloria lunae primogenitus&quot; or something that actually has correct Latin grammar).</p>
<p>I feel like the section on plants could be streamlined, as I found it a significant speedbump while reading. Perhaps:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>When initially discovered, the plant species <em>Amaranthus blitum</em> (purple amaranth) and <em>Cupressus sempervirens</em> (Mediterranean cypress) were found in SCP-2248. Despite SCP-2248 possessing suitable conditions for plant growth, seeds from at least 20 other species were found ungerminated. Radiocarbon dating indicates that the oldest seeds present are dated to circa 200 – 260 CE.<sup>1</sup></p>
<p>Plant species from outside the genus <em>Amaranthus</em> and <em>Cupressus</em> that are replanted into SCP-2248 show no signs of growth and will gradually wither. Notably, plants under the genus <em>Myrtus</em> and <em>Laurus</em> and their seeds will undergo spontaneous combustion within 24 hours of being placed into SCP-2248.</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
</div>
